/*****************************************************************************
    T1 -- SAME ACTION, RECURSING, WITH CURSORS THAT GENUINELY COEXIST.

    STRENGTHENED 2026-07-29 to answer a fair doubt. The first version walked a
    FLAT list, so every inner frame bottomed out immediately and its loop never
    ran -- two live cursors never existed at once, which is precisely the case a
    shared implementation could pass by accident.

    This one nests. trunk holds leafA and leafB; each leaf holds two entries. So
    while walk(leafA) is running its OWN loop to completion, trunk's cursor must
    sit untouched and then resume at leafB. That is emitPlan's shape.

    Still self-terminating: i, j, k, l carry no list, so the third level bottoms
    out on the no-list guard. No counter needed -- though iterAdvance now has a
    loud runaway abort regardless (EXCEEDED at 100000 advances).

    PER-FRAME expects EXACTLY, and in this order:
        at trunk / at leafA / at i / at j / at leafB / at k / at l      = 7
    Any sharing breaks the ORDER, not just the count: trunk resuming at leafB
    after a completed nested walk is the whole assertion.
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);

search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests bcOPs Utilities list;

register(IterTest);
define
    leafA   i j;
    leafB   k l;
    trunk   leafA leafB;

    walk argument code={
        print "at " taG argument:;
        iterate grup on argument;
        while ++grup
            walk(grup);
        };
    ;

walk(trunk);
stop();
